<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter13_composition_building_objects_from_objects">
    <title>Composition: Building Objects from Other Objects</title>

    <p>
        <term>Composition</term> means that a class contains one or more objects of
        other classes as data members. The containing object uses those component
        objects to carry out its work. Composition is a direct implementation of a
        has-a relationship.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_component_objects">
        <title>Objects as Components</title>

        <p>
            A component is an ordinary data member whose type is another class. In
            this example, a <c>Car</c> has an <c>Engine</c>. The <c>Car</c> is not a
            kind of <c>Engine</c>; it uses an engine as one of its parts.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_car_engine_composition">
            <title>A car delegates starting to its engine</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_car_engine_composition"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Engine {
private:
    std::string model;

public:
    Engine(const std::string& starting_model)
        : model{starting_model} {
    }

    void start() const {
        std::cout << model << " started\n";
    }
};

class Car {
private:
    Engine engine;

public:
    Car(const std::string& engine_model)
        : engine{engine_model} {
    }

    void start_car() const {
        engine.start();
        std::cout << "Car started\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Car car{"E-17"};
    car.start_car();

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The <c>Car</c> constructor initializes its <c>engine</c> member in the
            member-initializer list. The <c>start_car()</c> function then delegates
            part of its work to <c>engine.start()</c>.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_component_lifetime">
        <title>Component Lifetime</title>

        <p>
            When a component is stored as an ordinary data member, its lifetime is
            tied to the containing object. The component is constructed as part of
            the containing object and is destroyed when the containing object is
            destroyed.
        </p>

        <p>
            Construction follows the order of member declarations, not the order in
            which members appear in the constructor's initializer list. Destruction
            occurs in the reverse order. This rule ensures that a component remains
            available for the containing object throughout the containing object's
            lifetime.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_composition_lifetime">
            <title>Composition and component lifetime</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Design</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Component storage</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Component lifetime</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Typical meaning</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Composition</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Object member</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Usually tied to the containing object</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A car has an engine</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Reference to another object</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Reference member or parameter</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Managed elsewhere</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A report uses a formatter</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Inheritance</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Base-class subobject</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Part of the derived object</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A circle is a shape</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_delegation">
        <title>Delegating Work to Components</title>

        <p>
            A containing class can provide a simple public operation while allowing
            a component to perform specialized work. This separation lets each
            class focus on one responsibility. The containing class coordinates the
            operation, and the component class implements its own behavior.
        </p>

        <p>
            Composition does not prevent a component from changing later. For
            example, a car could contain a different engine class in a future design,
            as long as the car's expected interface remains clear. This flexibility
            is one reason composition is often preferred for combining implementation
            details.
        </p>

        <p>
            The following visualization shows the <c>Car</c> object containing its
            <c>Engine</c> component and delegating the start operation.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter13_composition"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%23include%20%3Cstring%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Engine%20%7B%0Aprivate%3A%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Astring%20model%3B%0A%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Engine%28const%20std%3A%3Astring%26%20starting_model%29%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%3A%20model%7Bstarting_model%7D%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20start%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20model%20%3C%3C%20%22%20started%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aclass%20Car%20%7B%0Aprivate%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Engine%20engine%3B%0A%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Car%28const%20std%3A%3Astring%26%20engine_model%29%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%3A%20engine%7Bengine_model%7D%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20start_car%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20engine.start%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Car%20started%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Car%20car%7B%22E-17%22%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20car.start_car%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=400&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises_chapter13_composition">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_composition_1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statement best describes the relationship between
                    <c>Car</c> and <c>Engine</c>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>A car is a specialized kind of engine.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The example does not model an is-a relationship.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A car contains an engine and uses it as a component.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This describes a has-a relationship implemented with composition.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>An engine inherits all members from a car.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The component relationship does not reverse the roles.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>A car and an engine must be the same class.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Composition combines objects of different classes.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_composition_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statements correctly describe composition? Select all
                    that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A containing object can delegate work to a component.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Delegation lets each class focus on its own responsibility.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Composition always means that the two classes have an is-a relationship.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Composition normally represents a has-a relationship.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>An object member can have a lifetime tied to its containing object.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This is a common form of composition.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Composition can reduce dependence on a class hierarchy.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The containing class uses a component instead of becoming its derived class.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_composition_2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    When a <c>Car</c> object is constructed, when is its
                    <c>Engine</c> member constructed?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The <c>Engine</c> member is constructed as part of constructing
                    the <c>Car</c>, before the body of the <c>Car</c> constructor
                    runs.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_composition_parsons"
                  number="yes"
                  adaptive="yes"
                  indentation="show">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Arrange the blocks to define a <c>Car</c> that initializes its
                    <c>Engine</c> component.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <blocks randomize="yes">
                <block order="1">
                    <cline>class Car {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="2">
                    <cline>private:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3">
                    <cline>    Engine engine;</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="4">
                    <cline>public:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="5">
                    <cline>    Car(const std::string&amp; model)</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="6">
                    <cline>        : engine{model} {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="7">
                    <cline>    }</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="8">
                    <cline>};</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="5" correct="no">
                    <cline>    Car(const std::string&amp; model) {</cline>
                </block>
            </blocks>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>
